The '''Republic of Guajicutea (1723–1768)''' covers the first four decades of the republic established by the [[Peaceful Revolution of 1723]], in which [[Sarta]] peacefully ceded control of its colony to the [[Padres de Guajicutea]]. The period encompasses the drafting of the constitution, the establishment of the senate and judiciary, the adoption of {{w|civil law (legal system)|civil law}}, and the consolidation of a rigid class structure with Sartans at the top. The economy shifted from plantation agriculture to light manufacturing as {{w|chattel slavery|slave-labor}} plantations in [[Lacashe]] outcompeted Guajicutean producers. The republic seized the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] from the [[Companyia Reial del Capuyaquiran]] in 1761, formed the [[Capuyaquiran and Abyalan League]] in 1764 to combat piracy, and weathered peasant rebellions in the 1760s that culminated in the constitutional reform of 1768, which expanded voting rights to all men and doubled the senate.

== Establishment of the Republic (1723) ==

The [[Peaceful Revolution of 1723]] transferred sovereignty from the [[Sarta|Sartan]] crown to the [[Padres de Guajicutea]], the founding group of Sartan-Guaji landowners, merchants, and intellectuals who had organised the republican movement. The transfer was negotiated through diplomatic channels, and no Sartan military force was deployed to oppose it.

The first task of the new government was the drafting of a constitution. The document, ratified in late 1723, established a senate elected by landowning men, a [[Cónsul]] as head of state charged with international affairs, and an independent judiciary operating under a {{w|civil law (legal system)|civil law}} code adapted from Sartan legal traditions. The Cónsul served a four-year term with no term limits, though a political tradition of single terms emerged almost immediately: the first Cónsul, [[Don Vicente de la Vega]], served one term and stepped aside, establishing a precedent that most of his successors followed.

[[Don Vicente de la Vega]]'s single term (1723–1727) was devoted to institutional construction. The constitution's framers divided the republic into sixteen ''[[Districts of the Republic of Guajicutea|districtos]]'' based on the former feudal bikabás, each electing one senator. The judiciary was organised as a tiered system of local courts in each distrito, with a high court in [[Tabakurí]] serving as the final appellate jurisdiction. The civil law code codified property rights, commercial obligations, and criminal penalties, replacing the patchwork of Sartan colonial decrees and residual Guaji customary practice that had governed the colony under Sartan rule.

== Government and districtos ==

The republic's governmental structure reflected the interests of the landowning elite who had engineered the transition. The senate consisted of sixteen senators, one per distrito, elected by landowning men. The Cónsul, elected by the senate, handled foreign affairs and served as the republic's representative in international relations. The suffrage restriction to landowners effectively confined political power to the Sartan and Sartan-Guaji mixed elite, who held the vast majority of registered land through the royal land sales of the 1710s and 1720s.

The sixteen districtos retained the territorial boundaries of the former feudal bikabás but used Sartan provincial names assigned during the colonial period. The continuity of territorial divisions from the feudal period to the republican period provided a measure of administrative stability, but it also preserved the geographic basis of the old landowning families' power.

The Cónsul's four-year term and the tradition of single terms produced a rapid turnover of executive leadership. Between 1723 and 1768, approximately eleven Cónsuls served, the majority for a single term. The tradition was not codified in the constitution — term limits were explicitly rejected — but the political culture of the Padres de Guajicutea and their successors treated voluntary retirement after one term as a mark of republican virtue.

== Society and class structure ==

The republic formalised a class structure that had been taking shape since the Sartan colonial period. At the apex stood the Sartan landowning elite, the families who had purchased royal lands and dominated the senate and the Cónsul's office. Below them sat a Sartan-Guaji mixed class of merchants, professionals, and colonial administrators who had integrated into the Sartan cultural sphere through education, language, and intermarriage. The Guaji native population — the descendants of the island's pre-colonial inhabitants — formed the rural majority, working as farmers, fishermen, and labourers on lands owned by others. The [[Kaftians]], descendants of enslaved people brought during the Burawi colonial period, had been freed but remained economically marginalised, concentrated in agricultural labour and domestic service. The [[Bayani]] migrants, who had arrived during the Sartan colonial period to work the fields, occupied a similar position at the base of the social hierarchy.

The class structure was rigid but not legally codified. No statute explicitly barred Guaji, Kaftians, or Bayani from holding office or owning land. The barriers were economic and cultural: suffrage was tied to land ownership, and land ownership was concentrated in Sartan hands. Sartan was the language of government, law, and education, and fluency in Sartan was a practical prerequisite for political participation. The Guaji language survived in rural communities but carried no political weight in the republic's institutions.

Over the four decades of this period, the class structure hardened rather than softened. The Sartan elite consolidated landholdings through inheritance, marriage, and purchase. The mixed Sartan-Guaji class grew as a result of intermarriage and cultural assimilation, but the lower strata of the hierarchy — Guaji natives, Kaftians, and Bayani — remained effectively excluded from political representation despite their growing economic importance as the workforce behind the republic's light manufacturing sector.

== Education and religion ==

The [[Petran Catholic|Sartan Petran Catholic Church]] was the republic's primary educational institution. Beginning in the 1730s, parish schools in Tabakurí, Waribá, and Barikú offered basic literacy instruction alongside religious doctrine. The programme expanded gradually into rural areas in the 1740s and 1750s, as parish priests established schools in village centres throughout the districtos. By the 1760s, a significant minority of the rural population had acquired basic literacy in Sartan.

The spread of education produced unintended political consequences. Peasants who learned to read encountered not only religious texts but also secular literature circulating through trade networks — political pamphlets, merchant correspondence, and accounts of republican governance that emphasised the principle of popular sovereignty. The contradiction between the republican ideals taught in the schools and the exclusionary reality of the landowner suffrage system contributed to political mobilisation among the rural population.

The Church's educational mission and its support for the political order created tensions within the institution. Parish priests, many of Guaji descent, reported that their students were increasingly questioning why the republic's promise of self-governance did not extend to them. The Church hierarchy, closely tied to the landowning elite, was reluctant to endorse political reform, but individual priests became sympathetic to the concerns of their congregations.

A syncretic Guaji Catholicism, developed during the colonial period, continued to thrive among the majority population. The triad of sea, mountain, and sky mapped onto the Holy Trinity, and cremation persisted as the dominant funeral rite despite Church preference for interment. The republic's official religion was Sartan Petran Catholic, but the lived religion of most Guajicuteans was a hybrid that the state tolerated but did not fully endorse.

== Culture and literature ==

The period saw a flourishing of cultural and literary production, as Sartan-language literature by Guaji-born authors emerged as a distinct tradition. The transcription of the [[Kari Yabí]] chronicle into Sartan, completed during the colonial period circa the 1650s, established a precedent for literary work that blended Guaji oral traditions with Sartan written forms. By the 1740s, a generation of Guaji-born, Sartan-educated authors was producing histories, poetry, and political commentary that drew on both cultural traditions.

An intellectual circle in Tabakurí, sometimes called the Guajicutean Enlightenment, absorbed {{w|Age of Enlightenment|Enlightenment}} ideas circulating through trade with [[Mercia]], [[Breisland]], and [[Lacashe]]. These ideas — popular sovereignty, natural rights, the social contract — provided the intellectual framework for both the republican movement and the later peasant rebellions that challenged the republic's exclusionary order.

The port cities of Waribá and Barikú developed distinct cultural characters. Waribá, the larger port and the site of the Astillero Real, attracted sailors, merchants, and artisans from across the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}} and beyond, producing a cosmopolitan culture that blended Sartan, Guaji, and increasingly Zong influences. Barikú, smaller and more military in character, remained closer to its colonial origins.

== Economic development ==

The republic inherited a plantation economy based on sugar, tobacco, and tropical dyes. In the early decades, plantation agriculture remained profitable, supported by trade with [[Volisania|Volisanian]] viceroyalties and [[Mercia]]. But the republic's plantations, worked by paid labour, could not match the productivity of [[Lacashe]]'s slave-labor estates, which produced cotton and sugar at costs no free-labour economy could match. As Lacashe's autonomous parliament expanded output through the 1740s and 1750s, Guajicutean plantation exports became less competitive in regional markets.

The economic response was a gradual shift toward light manufacturing. Sugar refining, tobacco curing, and food processing — activities that added value to raw agricultural output — expanded from the 1740s onward. Textile production, using imported raw cotton, developed in Waribá and Tabakurí. Metalworking, drawing on the pre-colonial copper and bronze traditions, supplied tools, hardware, and fittings for the shipyard. The shift was uneven: landowners who had invested in plantations resisted the transition, while merchant-manufacturers in the port cities embraced it.

The seizure of the [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] in 1761 accelerated the manufacturing shift. The shipyard, with its dry docks, workshops, and trained workforce, became the republic's largest industrial installation. Ship repair and construction generated demand for timber, rope, sailcloth, iron fittings, and skilled labour, creating a cluster of supporting industries around Waribá harbor.

Population grew from approximately 80,000 at the republic's founding to approximately 115,000 by 1768. Growth was driven by natural increase, continued Bayani migration, and the arrival of Sartan and Zong merchants. The population remained predominantly rural, but the urban centres of Tabakurí, Waribá, and Barikú grew steadily.

== Seizure of the Astillero Real (1761) ==

The [[Astillero Real de Waribá]] had been operated by the [[Companyia Reial del Capuyaquiran]], a [[Volisania|Volisanian]] royal trading company, since the Treaty of 1705. The company controlled the shipyard, the port facilities at Waribá, and a significant share of the colony's maritime commerce. The arrangement was a source of friction between the republic and the company from the outset: the republic's sovereignty extended nominally over Waribá, but its primary industrial asset was in Volisanian hands.

By the late 1750s, the [[Volisanian Civil War]] (1736–1801) had weakened Volisanian authority throughout its colonial possessions. The viceroyalties of [[Balisca]], [[Balboa]], and [[Tapuya]] operated semi-autonomously, and the Companyia Reial's capacity to enforce its claims in the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}} was diminished. The Cónsul [[Joaquín Delgado]] (1759–1763) calculated that Volisania could not respond to a seizure.

In 1761, the republic nationalised the Astillero Real and its associated port facilities, expelling the Companyia Reial's management and placing the shipyard under a newly created naval ministry. The Companyia Reial, whose parent state was consumed by civil war, issued a formal protest but could deploy no force. The seizure was popular domestically — the shipyard was seen as a colonial asset rightfully belonging to the republic — and it established a precedent for the republic's willingness to act unilaterally in matters of sovereignty.

== Piracy and the Capuyaquiran and Abyalan League (1764) ==

The Volisanian Civil War and the Burawi departure from the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}} created a security vacuum. Volisanian naval patrols, which had suppressed piracy under the colonial order, ceased to operate as the civil war consumed the fleet. Burawi naval forces, withdrawn to the Abyalan coast after the Imperial Split, no longer policed the sea lanes. Piracy surged in the 1740s and 1750s, disrupting trade routes between Guajicutea, Lacashe, and the Volisanian viceroyalties.

The republic's naval capacity was limited. The Waribá squadron inherited from the Sartan colonial period was small and aging. Coastal patrols could protect the immediate approaches to Waribá and Barikú, but the trade routes to the east and south were increasingly vulnerable. Merchant vessels suffered losses to pirates operating from uninhabited islands and hidden coves throughout the archipelago.

In 1764, the [[Capuyaquiran and Abyalan League]] was formed as a collective security alliance. The founding members were the [[Republic of Guajicutea]], the autonomous [[Province of Lacashe|Lacashe]] republic, the [[Asikyira|Republic of Asikyira]] (independent since 1743), and the former Burawi Capuyaquiran possessions that had gained independence in 1755. The league's primary purpose was anti-piracy: coordinated naval patrols, shared intelligence on pirate movements, and joint courts for the prosecution of captured pirates. The league also served as a diplomatic forum for the independent states of the region, providing a framework for collective action that none could undertake alone.

The league's formation marked a shift in the republic's geopolitical orientation. Where the colonial order had been defined by the rivalries of distant empires, the post-colonial order was shaped by the cooperation and competition of small independent states in a region destabilised by the Volisanian Civil War and the Burawi withdrawal.

== Peasant rebellions (1760s) ==

The spread of education to the rural population contributed to the political mobilisation of the lower strata. By the early 1760s, literate peasants and rural labourers — Guaji, Kaftian, and Bayani — were organising to demand political representation. The [[Rebelión de los Pueblos]] (1762–1763) was the first major peasant rebellion. Centred in the rural districtos of the interior, the rebellion involved armed bands of peasants who seized estate buildings, refused to pay rents, and demanded the extension of the franchise. The revolt was suppressed by the republic's small military force, but its scale prompted the senate to consider reform.

The [[Rebelión de Tabakurí]] (1766) was a larger and more direct challenge to the government. Rebels marched on the capital itself, occupying the distrito courthouse and the cathedral square for several days before being dispersed by troops from the Waribá garrison. The rebellion's leaders — literate peasants, former parish school students — presented a list of demands that included universal male suffrage, the doubling of the senate to ensure representation for non-landowning communities, and the appointment of judges from outside the landowning class.

The two rebellions, though suppressed, placed pressure on the landowner suffrage system. The senate, under Cónsul [[Diego Alvarado]] (1767–1771), began negotiations for constitutional reform. The reform bloc in the senate, led by senators from the more commercially oriented districtos who saw the manufacturing economy's dependence on a broader workforce, argued that extending the franchise would stabilise the republic and integrate the lower strata into the political order.

== Foreign relations ==

The republic's closest ally was [[Lacashe]], the autonomous Burawi parliamentary republic on the Abyalan coast. From 1723 until Asikyira's independence in 1743, Guajicutea and Lacashe were the only independent states in the {{w|Capuyaquiran Sea}} region. The two republics maintained close trade relations, diplomatic correspondence, and intellectual exchange. Lacashe's parliamentary system, with its landowner suffrage and planter-dominated legislature, served as both a political model and a point of comparison for Guajicutean republicans.

Relations with [[Asikyira]] were established after its independence in 1743. The two republics shared an interest in regional stability and anti-piracy cooperation, though the distance between them limited the depth of the relationship. Asikyira's mixed Burawi-indigenous character and its indenture-based economy contrasted with Guajicutea's Sartan-Guaji colonial heritage, but both were post-colonial republics navigating a region dominated by larger powers.

Relations with [[Breisland]] soured during this period. Breisland had been a key trade partner during the Sartan colonial period, supplying the colony from [[Lurandia]] during the trade embargo. After the republic's seizure of the Astillero Real in 1761, Breisland, which had commercial ties to the Companyia Reial, issued a formal protest and imposed trade restrictions on Guajicutean vessels calling at Breislandic ports. The dispute was compounded by Breislandic ships being implicated in piracy in the Capuyaquiran Sea — privateers nominally loyal to Breisland but operating without effective supervision during the Volisanian Civil War. By the mid-1760s, Breislandic-Guajicutean trade had declined significantly.

As relations with Breisland deteriorated, the republic cultivated closer ties with the [[Zong|Zongosphere]]. Zong emissaries arrived in Waribá in 1765, seeking trade partners as Volisania's capacity to manage its colonial empire diminished. The emissaries offered access to Zong manufactured goods, textiles, and porcelain on terms more favourable than the Breislandic trade. The senate, over Breislandic objections, welcomed the Zong initiative, and by the late 1760s, Zong goods were flowing through Waribá in increasing volumes.

The [[Volisanian Civil War|Volisanian]] viceroyalties of [[Balisca]], [[Balboa]], and [[Tapuya]] operated semi-autonomously throughout this period, trading directly with Guajicutea without reference to the Volisanian crown. The viceroyalties were not independent — they remained nominally subject to Volisania — but the civil war had severed effective metropolitan control. Trade with the viceroyalties provided an important market for Guajicutean manufactured goods and agricultural products, and a source of raw materials and luxury goods.

In 1755, [[Burawa]] granted independence to its remaining Capuyaquiran possessions — the islands it had conquered during the colonial era. The republic courted the newly independent islands to join the Republic of Guajicutea, offering representation in the senate and access to the republic's economic infrastructure. None accepted besides the small island of [[Warikú]], which joined in 1756. The other islands preferred to maintain their independence, though most joined the Capuyaquiran and Abyalan League in 1764.

== Constitutional reform of 1768 ==

The peasant rebellions and the growing political awareness of the lower strata produced a reform movement within the senate. Cónsul [[Diego Alvarado]], elected in 1767, championed a package of constitutional amendments that expanded the franchise to all adult men, regardless of land ownership, and doubled the senate to two representatives per distrito. The reform was debated through 1767 and early 1768, with opposition from the most conservative landowning senators and support from the commercial and reformist blocs.

The amendments were ratified in mid-1768. The expansion of the senate to thirty-two senators — two per distrito — was designed to ensure that the newly enfranchised population would have representation from candidates who could compete for the second seat in each distrito without displacing the existing landowning representative. The reform did not extend suffrage to women, Kaftians, or Bayani who did not own property — the franchise was expanded to all men, but the definition of "man" in the civil law code was construed narrowly, and the property qualification was removed only for men.

The reform of 1768 marked the end of the first phase of the republic. The landowner republic of the Padres de Guajicutea gave way to a broader political order, though one that retained significant exclusions. The tensions that the reform addressed — class exclusion, economic inequality, and the gap between republican ideals and republican practice — continued to shape the republic's subsequent development.

